    "angle": "You don't need a big, dramatic moment to hear your heart. It is already speaking in five ordinary situations: a mean group-chat joke, a broken pen, a post that isn't really you, a raised hand, and money on the floor when no one is watching.",
    "audience_track": "child_teen",
    "format": "carousel",
    "slide_count": 7,
    "cta_intent": "save",
    "rationale": "First post in the pipeline, so there is no recent history to rotate against. This atom is the strongest carousel candidate in the bank for the child_teen track: five concrete situations a 11-15 year old recognises, each with a built-in contrast (what the mind says vs what the heart says) and a closing question, all verbatim from the workbook so grounding is safe. The atom is not sensitive. CTA is 'save' (a framework is something to come back to); discover_book is deliberately not used on the very first post, per MASTER-STRATEGY.",
    "considered_alternatives": [
      {
        "atom_id": "bt_ch04_head_vs_heart_decisions",
        "why_not": "A 5-row table of abstract contrasts; one row ('What do you know is inside') reads awkwardly out of context. The situation-based atom is more concrete."
      },
      {
        "atom_id": "bt_ch08_world_needs_best_version_of_you",
        "why_not": "Strong single quote, but better saved for a later post so the first one demonstrates the carousel template and the mind-vs-heart idea that the rest of Section 4 builds on."
      }
    ]
  },
  "copywriter": {
    "stage": "copywriter",
    "post_id": "post_20260921_01",
    "atom_id": "bt_ch04_heart_moments_daily_life",
    "revision": 0,
    "hook": "Your heart is already talking.",
    "headline": "Your heart speaks up in small, everyday moments.",
    "hook_family": "direct_address",
    "slides": [
      {
        "n": 1,
        "role": "hook",
        "kicker": "Ordinary moments",
        "headline": "Your heart is already talking.",
        "text": "Five everyday moments where you can hear it.",
        "note": "Swipe to hear it",
        "emphasis_text": "talking"
      },
      {
        "n": 2,
        "role": "example",
        "headline": "Join in, or speak up?",
        "text": "You’re in a group chat, and someone sends a mean joke about another kid.\n\n→ Mind: “Just laugh. Don’t make it a big deal.”\n→ Heart: “That’s not fair. I wouldn’t want this done to me.”",
        "emphasis_text": "speak up"
      },
      {
        "n": 3,
        "role": "example",
        "headline": "Can you stay kind, even when angry?",
        "text": "Your younger sibling accidentally breaks your favorite pen.\n\n→ Mind: “Yell! That was special!”\n→ Heart: “She didn’t mean to. Remember when you broke Dad’s glasses once?”",
        "emphasis_text": "kind"
      },
      {
        "n": 4,
        "role": "example",
        "headline": "Is this true to who I am?",
        "text": "You want to post something that’ll get likes — but deep down, it’s not really you.\n\nMaybe it’s showing off or copying someone else.",
        "emphasis_text": "true"
      },
      {
        "n": 5,
        "role": "example",
        "headline": "Be brave and speak, or stay silent and safe?",
        "text": "You know the answer, but you’re afraid to raise your hand.\n\nYou’re worried someone might laugh if you're wrong.",
        "emphasis_text": "brave"
      },
      {
        "n": 6,
        "role": "example",
        "headline": "What will you do when no one will ever know?",
        "text": "You find money on the floor. There’s no one around. No cameras.\n\n→ Mind: “Keep it.”\n→ Heart: “Someone probably needs this.”",
        "emphasis_text": "no one"
      },
      {
        "n": 7,
        "role": "takeaway",
        "headline": "You don’t need a big moment.",
        "text": "Most of the time, your heart shows up in the small, everyday choices — the ones that happen in seconds, when no one is watching.",
        "emphasis_text": "small"
      }
    ],
    "caption": "Your heart doesn’t announce itself. It shows up in tiny moments: a mean joke in the group chat, a broken pen, a post that isn’t really you, a hand you almost keep down.\n\nThat quiet voice is calm, kind and steady, and it can say something different from whatever your mind says is the fastest, smartest move.\n\nWhich of these five moments sounds most like your week?\n\nSave this for the next time you’re not sure what to do.",
    "hashtags": [
      "#SocialEmotionalLearning",
      "#SEL",
      "#EmotionalIntelligence",
      "#SelfAwareness",
      "#InnerVoice",
